+## Python 3.11 parse pass
+
+`python3 -m compileall` under Python 3.11 failed on 31 of the scripts, all for the same
+reason and none of them a real bug: `[tool.ruff] target-version = "py312"` lets the formatter
+split a long f-string **expression** across physical lines, which only parses on Python 3.12+
+(PEP 701). Example of what it produced:
+
+```python
+f"[DRY RUN] Would compress & {
+    'remove' if remove_original else 'keep'
+}: {input_path} -> {output_path}"
+```
+
+- **30 files fixed mechanically** — each split expression was joined back onto one line. Only
+  whitespace *inside* `{...}` changed, so the rendered strings are byte-identical; every diff was
+  verified to be whitespace-only and every file recompiled afterwards.
+- **1 file fixed by hand** — `html_template_gen/make_template_html.py` built its table of contents
+  with a generator expression spread over six lines *inside* a triple-quoted f-string, so that
+  moved out into a `toc` variable computed before the template.
+- **1 file needed nothing** — `remove_unused_code/afkco.py` was deleted in the dedup round above.
+- **Result: all 1209 scripts now parse under Python 3.11** (`compileall` is clean) and still parse
+  under 3.12/3.13.
+- Heads-up: running `ruff format` again with `target-version = "py312"` will re-split those
+  f-strings. Set `target-version = "py311"` in `pyproject.toml` if the 3.11 compatibility should
+  survive formatting.
