+ck "default is non-recursive for an ext target" "$(python3 "$B/run_external_formatter/run_formatter.py" .svg echo 2>&1 | grep -c 'Processed:')" "2"
+ck "extension without the leading dot works" "$(python3 "$B/run_external_formatter/run_formatter.py" svg echo 2>&1 | grep -c 'Processed:')" "2"
+ck "shell target: .sh + extensionless shebang, binaries skipped" "$(python3 "$B/run_external_formatter/run_formatter.py" shell echo 2>&1 | grep -c 'Processed:')" "1"
+ck "--no-skip-binary lets the binary .sh through" "$(python3 "$B/run_external_formatter/run_formatter.py" --no-skip-binary shell echo 2>&1 | grep -c 'Processed:\|Failed:')" "2"
+ck "--shebang python adds the extensionless rule for p.py" "$(python3 "$B/run_external_formatter/run_formatter.py" --shebang python shell echo 2>&1 | grep -c 'Processed:')" "1"
+has "no match message" "$(python3 "$B/run_external_formatter/run_formatter.py" .nothing echo 2>&1)" "No .nothing files"
+has "missing CLI reported as a failure" "$(python3 "$B/run_external_formatter/run_formatter.py" .svg definitely-not-installed 2>&1)" "Summary: 0 successful, 2 failed"
+ck "--failures-only shows only the failures" "$(python3 "$B/run_external_formatter/run_formatter.py" --failures-only .svg definitely-not-installed 2>&1 | grep -c '❌')" "2"
+cd "$W" || exit 1
+
+echo
+echo "================================ TOTAL: $pass passed, $fail failed  (scratch: $W)"
+rm -rf "$W"
+[ "$fail" -eq 0 ]
diff --git a/arena/README.md b/arena/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..54448c7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arena/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,86 @@
+# arena — the tooling that processed this repo
+
+This directory is **not** part of the script collection; it is the code that was used to
+reorganise it, kept so the work is reproducible. Everything here is excluded from
+`SCRIPT_INDEX.md`, from the duplicate scans and from the folder statistics (the `SKIP`
+sets in those tools name `arena`).
+
+Two steps were run on the repo:
+
+| step | what happened | tools |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **1. folderize** | 1238 `.py` files sitting flat in a Termux `bin/` dir, plus 1240 extension-less symlinks that mirrored each script name, became 161 folders grouped by job (fine-grained on purpose, so near-duplicates land next to each other). The symlinks were deleted: they pointed at absolute Termux paths and were broken duplicates. Scripts with a unique job stayed in the repo root. | `01_folderize/` |
+| **2. merge** | three sub-rounds: (a) collapse the 21 pairs that were ≥ 95 % the same program, (b) collapse the 15 of the remaining ≥ 90 % pairs that turned out to be the same job twice, (c) make every script parse on Python 3.11 as well as 3.12+. Then, folder by folder, replace all the scripts in a folder with **one** merged script whose flags expose every difference between the originals. | `02_merge/` |
+
+## Provenance labels
+
+Every file starts with a `PROVENANCE:` line, because the sandbox was **re-cloned**
+mid-job: `/tmp` (where the throwaway heredoc scripts lived) was wiped, and with it the
+git objects of four local commits. The worktree survived, so the results are all still
+here, but the tools had to be recovered in two different ways:
+
+- **real** — the code that actually ran, reproduced verbatim apart from a docstring, a
+  CLI and (where noted) a bug fix. This covers everything written after the re-clone,
+  plus the f-string transformer and the index generator, which were rewritten from the
+  exact source that had been run.
+- **reconstructed** — the same job, rewritten from what the tool demonstrably did (its
+  output and its effects on the repo are recorded in the commit messages and in
+  `SCRIPT_INDEX.md`). The step-1 tools are in this category; their real input, the
+  1179-entry name → folder mapping, was recovered from git instead and is checked in as
+  `01_folderize/folder_map.json`.
+
+## `01_folderize/` — step 1
+
+| file | what it is |
+| --- | --- |
+| `dump_features.py` | *reconstructed.* Extracts the "what does this script do" features of every file: shebang, first docstring line, stdlib / third-party / local imports, argparse flags, referenced file extensions, external commands it shells out to, whether it writes files, whether it uses multiprocessing. 783 of the 1238 scripts had no docstring, which is why the taxonomy was designed from this vector rather than from documentation. `--no-docstring-only` dumps just those. |
+| `folderize.py` | *reconstructed.* Applies a mapping: create the folders, move the scripts, delete the mirrored symlinks, then enforce the "no folder with a single member" rule by demoting that script back to the root. `--dry-run` prints the whole plan. Its first version crashed with `FileNotFoundError` because a folder-set comprehension iterated `cnt.values()` (ints) instead of `cnt.items()`; fixed here. |
+| `derive_folder_map.py` | *real.* Recovers the mapping step 1 applied straight out of git (`git diff --find-renames --name-status BASE HEAD`): R100 entries are the moves, D entries are the symlinks and later-deleted duplicates. Also snapshots the live layout. |
+| `folder_map.json` | *generated, checked in.* 1179 flat-name → folder assignments, the deleted and added paths, and the current worktree layout (161 folders, 1195 grouped scripts). |
+| `similarity_scan.py` | *real logic.* The near-duplicate scanner behind both dedup rounds: drop the shebang, collapse whitespace, then `difflib.SequenceMatcher(..., autojunk=False).ratio()`. **autojunk must be off** — with it on, ratios collapse on a long single-line file (pcssmin/pjsmin scored 89.2 instead of 92.6, clean_css/cleants 77.0 instead of 91.6) and the published numbers do not reproduce. Intra-folder by default, `--all-pairs` with a length prefilter for the whole repo. |
+| `verify_layout.py` | *reconstructed.* Post-conditions after any step: counts, root singletons, no folder below the member minimum (`--expect-merged` instead requires exactly one script per folder), no symlinks left, no empty files or folders, no committed `__pycache__`, and every file parses on the running interpreter. |
+
+## `02_merge/` — step 2
+
+| file | what it is |
+| --- | --- |
+| `fix_pep701_fstrings.py` | *real.* The transformer that made 30 files parse on Python 3.11. `ruff format` with `target-version = "py312"` splits a long f-string **expression** over several physical lines (PEP 701, 3.12+ only); this joins the expression back onto one line. Literal text is copied byte for byte, nested string literals too, and a whitespace run inside `{...}` collapses to `""` or `" "` depending on whether the two neighbouring characters would merge into one token (`'a'\nif x\nelse 'b'` → `'a' if x else 'b'`, never `'a'if xelse'b'`). Sites needing more than that (a comment or a nested f-string inside an expression) raise `Manual` and are reported instead of mangled — that is what flagged `html_template_gen/make_template_html.py` for its hand fix. `--check` reports without writing. |
+| `extract_fstring_regions.py` | *real.* Prints the exact source region of every f-string the interpreter cannot parse, using the same scanner — the dump that showed all 31 failures were one shape and therefore mechanically fixable. |
+| `compile_check.py` | *real logic.* Lists the files the running interpreter cannot parse, with the offending line and a summary of failure kinds. Documents the trap that produced a false "all 1222 files fail" report the first time: `py_compile.compile(..., cfile=os.devnull)` cannot write its output, so every file raises — the cfile must be a real path. |
+| `check_diff_whitespace_only.py` | *real.* The safety net for those joins: per changed file, concatenate the removed lines and the added lines, strip all whitespace from both, compare. Identical ⇒ only whitespace moved, so no character of any string literal was lost. (It cannot prove whitespace *inside* a literal survived — stripping hides that — which is why the joined lines were also read by eye.) |
+| `regen_index.py` | *real.* Regenerates `SCRIPT_INDEX.md`: folder listing biggest-first, the collapse logs, the remaining-pair report. It carries round 1's log forward by reading the existing file, and round 2's prose is embedded — that prose is the part to edit as the merge progresses. Slow (~8 min) because of the all-folder similarity scan; run it from the repo root when a milestone is reached, not per batch. |
+| `folder_stats.py` | *real.* Folder sizes, LOC per folder, the member-count distribution and the LOC buckets — the numbers that decided the batching order ("161 folders, 1204 scripts, 183,872 LOC, median 875"), and the "10 smallest remaining" list that picks each batch. `--skip-merged` hides folders already reduced to one script. |
+| `git_state_report.sh` | *real.* The exact commands that revealed the re-clone: HEAD back at the pre-folderization base, 2483 index entries against 1217 worktree `.py` files, and a reflog containing only `clone:` + `checkout:`. It also prints the recovery used afterwards, which is safe because it never touches the worktree: `git reset --soft main && git add -A && git commit`. |
+| `test_merged_scripts.sh` | *real.* ~110 assertions over the six batch-1 merged scripts whose dependencies exist in the sandbox (`run_formatter.py` is driven with `/bin/echo` as the external CLI). It caught three genuine bugs — `archive_and_clean.py --exclude` resolving against the cwd instead of the archived directory, `pickle_convert.py --report` being silenced by `--quiet`, and `merge_css.py --engine cleancss` dumping a raw `FileNotFoundError` traceback when clean-css is not installed (it now says so and suggests `--engine join`) — and its own wrong expectations are annotated inline (fresh sources per merge_css run, equal-length words for the near-duplicate scan, append-by-design `similar.txt`, `.sh` always matching `TARGET=shell`). `qr_decode.py` and `telegram_link_scraper.py` are not covered: PIL/pyzbar and telethon are not installed there. |
+
+## Running them
+
+```bash
+cd /path/to/bin_sep
+
+# step 1 record and checks
+python3 arena/01_folderize/derive_folder_map.py --report -o arena/01_folderize/folder_map.json
+python3 arena/01_folderize/dump_features.py . -o /tmp/features.jsonl --tsv /tmp/features.tsv
+python3 arena/01_folderize/verify_layout.py . --expect-merged
+python3 arena/01_folderize/folderize.py . --map arena/01_folderize/folder_map.json --dry-run
+python3 arena/01_folderize/similarity_scan.py . --threshold 0.90
+
+# step 2 tools
+python3 arena/02_merge/compile_check.py . --show-line --kind-summary
+python3 arena/02_merge/fix_pep701_fstrings.py . --check
+python3 arena/02_merge/extract_fstring_regions.py . -o /tmp/regions.txt
+python3 arena/02_merge/check_diff_whitespace_only.py --rev HEAD~1 HEAD
+python3 arena/02_merge/folder_stats.py . --skip-merged
+bash    arena/02_merge/git_state_report.sh .
+bash    arena/02_merge/test_merged_scripts.sh .
+```
+
+`folderize.py --dry-run` on the current tree correctly reports nothing to move: the
+mapping has already been applied, and only the 5 root singletons are left unmapped
+(they are meant to stay in the root).
+
+## Merge progress
+
+Batch 1 (the 8 smallest folders) is done: 17 scripts → 8 merged scripts, one per
+folder. 153 folders / 1187 scripts / ~183k LOC remain, to be taken smallest-first.
+`folder_stats.py --skip-merged` prints the next batch at any time.
